// Documented open parser defect (book-verification 2026-09-19, repros r_listlit / r_listlit3).
// A list literal of int64/string is fine (spec §3.3.4 `list_literal`), and a list literal of
// RECORD literals is fine too (r_listlit2: `[{ urgency: int64, animal: string }, ...]` compiles
// cleanly). A list literal whose elements are TUPLE literals does not: the parser accepts the
// first tuple element, then at the comma introducing the second element it reports E1040
// "expected ';' after this statement before the next statement" -- as if it had decided the
// list literal was actually finished (or was never a list literal) after the first `(...)` and
// is now parsing ordinary sequence statements. Same failure with any tuple element type
// (int64/int64 here; r_listlit used int64/string).
// Expected: xs has 2 elements, exit 2.
// Observed: E1040 at the comma after the first tuple element.

fn main() -> int64 {
    sequence proc[mem(normal)]
        xs: List[(int64, int64), mem(normal)] <- [(3, 4), (1, 2)]
    produces
        pure length[(int64, int64), mem(normal)](xs)
    end
}
